A||||10||David Gamache||88formula-I would really appreciate your opinion!||||5||
Z||000000||David Gamache||02-08-2001||12:26 AM||||I have been preparing to do a rebuild on one of my fiero GT's and have been collecting parts. I noticed you really did your homework on the 3.1& 3.4 issue and would value your thoughts.  I have picked up a 3.1L short block and was wondering if it would be better to rebuild my 2.8 with the addition of a 3.1 crank, use the 3.1 short block and bolt on the heads and accessories on to it from the 2.8 (changing the cam and pistons as recommended), or just leave the 2.8 stock and get rid of the 3.1 idea altogether? I am not trying to build a race motor only gain a little power. I am beginning to get a bit confused on what would be the best way to go. Thank you, David||||reg||5
Z||000001||Hank is Here||02-08-2001||12:42 AM||||I am not an expert but do believe that the 3.1 and 3.4 have a somewhat improved oiling system.  For the most part however I believe the engines would be the same between the two that you possibly could build.  My decision is what could be done cheaper, less machining costs?  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Standard||02-08-2001||02:04 AM||||All 60* v6's after 87 had an improved oiling system.  If you have a pre-87 that you're going to rebuild, you can get the improved oiling by adding lead-in grooves on the #2 and #3 crankshaft main bearing journals, the block's upper main bearing bores and adding an extra lube hole in the upper main bearing.  Try to track down a copy of "How to Rebuild Your GM V-6 60* Engine"  out of print, but very nice if you can find one.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Raydar||02-08-2001||09:53 AM||||Babbling deleted... Sorry.<p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 02-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||88formula||02-08-2001||08:21 PM||||I almost missed this post!<P>David, you can get allot of power form your 2.8-liter engine if done correctly. The main advantage of the 3.1 and 3.4-liter engines is torque. Those two engines develop more low-end torque and horsepower than your 2.8-liter does because of the increased stroke. <P>If you have the automatic transmission than I would want the bigger engine because the increased bottom end power will work great with an automatic transmission. If you have a manual transmission than a built up 2.8-liter engine is a good choice and can satisfy your needs. <P>Can you give me a better description of what exactly you are using this fiero for along with what kind of money you are willing to spend? If you give me all of the specifications about your fiero like transmission type and final drive ratio than I will give you some expected performance figures using Dyno2000 and Dragstrip2000 to let you know what to expect from either engine combination like I did for Eric. This way armed with this information you will be more educated on what engine is best for you and make the right decision based on your needs.  <BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||David Gamache||02-10-2001||01:08 AM||||88Formula, First, thank you for replying.  My fiero is an 85 GT that I had upgraded in all respects to an 87 style GT some time ago. The transmission is the GT Muncie 5-speed and the 2.8 is stock at this time. I have the 3.1L short block sitting in the garage from a 92, I believe it was a Grand Prix, which was stock.  I only intend to drive the car city/highway and wanted to get just a little more power out of the car if possible.  I do not mind spending some money to improve the car, but probably would not spent thousands upon thousands of dollars for a moderate gain.  This is why I had considered building a stroker motor 2.8 with the 3.1 crank ( I thought the compression might be better) or just bolting my existing 2.8 parts to the 3.1L block with a new cam and possibly better compression pistons. Any thing you could advise would help before I make a mess of things! I am afraid of building a motor with low compression and less power than I started with. I also have to keep the motor smog legal. Thanks David||||reg||1
Z||000006||88formula||02-10-2001||04:08 PM||||The 3.1-liter engine has a better oiling system than your 85 2.8-liter engine. With the 3.1-liter engine you would also gain the small amount of power and toque you are looking for just because of the longer stroke crankshaft (3.310 Vs 2.99).You probably know this but your 85 flywheel will not work on the 3.1-liter motor because your 85 motor is externally balanced and the 3.1-liter engine is internally balanced. As far as I know everything from your 2.8-liter engine including the heads will bolt up to the 3.1-liter engine with little or not modification. You can use the fieros fuel system and injectors too. Both the 2.8 and 3.1-liter engines have the same 3.5” bore. <P>The 3.1-liter engine you have though probably had the aluminum heads, which means the dish of your 3.1 pistons, is somewhere around 26cc according to one member here on the forum.  I don’t know for sure. The combustion chamber volume of the aluminum heads is much less than that of your fiero iron heads (one member here claims they are 28cc). I had my fiero heads cc’ed and they varied form one chamber to another with a minimum of 49.4 to a high of 51.5 with the SI stainless steel valves in them. With the fiero heads your compression ratio would be a dismal 7.13-1 if that were the 3.1 that has the aluminum heads.  If that is a 3.1-liter engine that had the iron heads than your compression ratio would be somewhere around 8.47 if the pistons have a 10.64cc dish like one member here claims they do and your deck height averages .016 inches from the top of the piston. If the dish measures 12cc like the Silvolite 1486 replacement pistons for that motor than your compression ratio would be around 8.2-8.4 depending on what your deck height, gasket thickness, gasket diameter, and head chamber volume actually is. I measured my used 2.8-liter gasket today and it was .035” thick with a 3.610 diameter. With that gasket, Silvolite 1486 pistons, a zero deck height from the top of the piston, a 51.5cc chamber volume your compression ratio would be 8.65-1.<P>The only pistons that I could find for the 3.1-liter engine are replacement pistons with a 12cc dish volume like the Silvolite 1486 pistons made for the iron head 3.1 engine. You could get custom pistons made but that is going to cost you. I looked into getting custom forged lightweight pistons for my 3.4 liter engine and it was going to cost me around $800-$1000. I decided to go with the cheaper Federal Mogul H684CP DOHC pistons, which will give me a 9.7-1 compression ratio with a zero deck height. The Federal Mogul pistons are not that good for quality and they weighed a heavy 513 grams for a 3.640 diameter hyereutectic piston. I wanted the custom forged pistons because they are stronger and would have weighed around 350 grams but I won’t pay $1000 for them. With such a light piston my motor would have revved more easily, made more power, and been easier on the main and rod bearings.<P>The camshaft that came with that motor might be a good camshaft for performance. You should degree the camshaft and measure the lift. If you get the camshaft specifications I could run them through Dyno2000 for you so you would know what kind of power to expect from that camshaft. I ran some numbers for you with Dyno2000 and dragstrip2000 so that you could see the kind of power you should get from this motor. <P>This is a 3.1-liter engine with stock fiero heads, Crane compucam 2030 (GM12363220 camshaft is not legal for California), ported exhaust fiero manifolds and performance exhaust system, 8.2 compression, and a .030” over bore. <BR>RPM Horsepower Torque<BR>2000 68 178<BR>2500 88 185<BR>3000 111 195<BR>3500 130 195<BR>4000 145 190<BR>4500 154 180<BR>5000 156 164<BR>5500 150 143<BR>6000 135 118<BR>6500 115 93<P>With that engine, a 2925 lb. weight including you, and the 5-speed transmission your fiero would go 0-60 mph in 7.3 seconds and run the quarter mile in 15.32 seconds @ 88.5 mph. The stock fiero goes 0-60 mph in 7.96 seconds and runs the quarter mile in 15.72 seconds @ 86 mph.  <P>If you had an 8.6-1 compression ratio your 0-60 times would improve to 7.1 seconds and you would run the quarter mile in 15.2 seconds @ 89.4 mph. <P>Since there are so many performance parts for the 2.8-liter engine you could build that engine up or build an 88 2.8-liter motor up with the better oiling system and get 9.5-1compression pistons.  Using the same combination of parts but just changing the stroke to 2.99 and bringing up the compression ratio to 9.5 your power would look like this.<BR>RPM Horsepower Torque<BR>2000 64 168<BR>2500 84 176<BR>3000 107 187<BR>3500 127 191<BR>4000 144 189<BR>4500 157 183<BR>5000 164 172<BR>5500 164 157<BR>6000 158 138 <BR>6500 146 118<P>With this 2.8-liter engine your 0-60 mph time would be 6.9 seconds and you would run the quarter mile in 15.02 seconds @ 91 mph.<P>With a built up stock 2.8-liter engine your power would look like this. I used stock heads, FOCOA headers, 8.5 compression, a stock camshaft, and a performance exhaust system like the Borla system.<BR>2000 72 189<BR>2500 92 193<BR>3000 112 195<BR>3500 131 197<BR>4000 147 193<BR>4500 158 184<BR>5000 160 168<BR>5500 153 146<BR>6000 138 120<BR>6500 118 95<P>With this motor your fiero will go 0-60 mph in 7.0 seconds and run the quarter mile in 15 seconds @ 89.2 mph.<P>You could also build up a 3.4-liter Camaro engine like I have and use the pistons I did to get much better performance but it would cost you more money than the 2.8 and 3.1-liter engines and you would have to do more modifications to make it work. With a 3.4-liter engine, stock heads, Crane 2030 cam, 9.7-1 compression, ported fiero exhaust manifolds with performance exhaust, and a .030 over bore you power would look like this.<BR>Rpm Horsepower Torque<BR>2000 79 208<BR>2500 102 214<BR>3000 127 223<BR>3500 148 222<BR>4000 164 215<BR>4500 173 202<BR>5000 174 183<BR>5500 166 159<BR>6000 149 131<BR>6500 128 103<P>With the 3.4-liter engine your car would go 0-60 mph in 6.5 seconds and run the quarter mile in 14.7 seconds @ 92 mph. <P>The advantage of a higher compression ratio other than the obvious power increase is that you also get better gas mileage, which may or may not be important to you. A performance exhaust system with either your ported manifolds or real headers will also improve your gas mileage.<P>To make decent power with the 3.1-liter engine you might want to look into getting custom pistons for it. With a compression ratio of around 8.2-8.6 the 3.1-liter engine would give you the worst gas mileage but it would be the easiest motor to rebuild since you already have it. If you rebuilt a 3.1 or 3.4-liter engine you wouldn’t have to put your fiero out of commission while you wait for the machine shop and parts like you would if you removed your 2.8-liter engine. You could rebuild your 2.8-liter engine but with its questionable oiling system that might not be the best way to go for you. If you could find a later design motor like an 88 and up 2.8-liter engine than you could build a strong and efficient engine with a 9.5-1 compression ratio. If I were you I would narrow my pick to either the built up 2.8-liter engine or the 3.4-liter engine. If you could get pistons for the 3.1-liter engine that would give you a higher compression ratio than it would be the way to go. It’s up to you. <P>There are modifications like an aluminum flywheel and the SI stainless steel valves that you could add to any of those engines to boost power and efficiency even more. I have the SI valves and am getting the aluminum flywheel for my 3.4-liter engine. The SI valves will flow more air than you stock valves and the aluminum flywheel will free up some more horsepower and allow your engine to respond quicker when you accelerate. <P>For machine work you should have your reciprocating assembly balanced. To help prevent detonation and increase your compression ratio at the same time you should 0-deck your block and square the deck. You should replace the rod and main bolts with ARP Rod and Main studs if you intend to use nitrous or just want a more reliable and a stronger bottom end. The GM fasteners are junk and so are their pistons?<P>With all this information I hope it is easier for you to make a decision now. <P><BR>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG">   <P><p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 02-10-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||David Gamache||02-10-2001||05:25 PM||||Your information is a great help for making an informed decision.  I wish I had this much concise information before I had purchased the 3.1L short block. Also, you were correct about the engine orginally having aluminum heads. I do not think it would be advantageous to spend a thousand or so dollars on a set of pistons, but that would be the proper way to do the 3.1L as you mentioned due to the low compression. I think I will consider locating a 3.4L engine and try to find out what years were the best choice and if there will be any problems with smog regulation. If that does not work out than I will look into doing a little block work on an 88 engine and add the higer compression pistons and such as you recommend. Well, looks like it is time to post an ad for sale on this 3.1 short block! Thank you again, for taking the time to clear up a lot of gray area on the engine swap/modifications. David ||||reg||1
Z||000008||88formula||02-10-2001||08:53 PM||||The 93-95 Chevy Camaro has the 3.4-liter ohv engine. There is a later generation 3.4-liter engine that has aluminum heads and a roller a roller camshaft and is used in GM minivans. Fierosound knows more about this engine than I do since he has built one up for his fiero.||||reg||1
Z||000009||tgowens||02-11-2001||02:34 AM||||you can use the 3.4L from a '93-'95 Camaro or Firebird - expect to pay around $650 for one if you find one. It is a fairly simple install. The swaps we do here at the shop we use those engines. Basically everything from the heads up from a stock 2.8 will fit. You will need to reposition the starter to the other side (we have the drill guide here at the shop) and we use Lucas 305 injectors with an adjustable fuel pressure regulator as well.......the hp is improved as well as torque.......<P>You do not want to use the 3.4's from the Grand Prix, etc - those are the front wheel drive versions - make sure it is the pushrod version from the rear wheel drive Camaro and Firebird - If you need any help with this, call us here at the shop - 510-233-4376.......or......drive on up the coast to Richmond and we can do the swap for you.........||||reg||1
Z||000010||ironhedno3||02-11-2001||11:32 AM||||go for broke...do a 3.4..its well worth it..if ya cant find anyone to relocate the starter..get a 96and up minivan block and use camaro/firebird pistons with the heads and intake from the fiero..its a good idea to use the bigger injector/and or an adjustable regulator..but i am using all stock fiero stuff without a problem  ||||reg||1
